@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}

<h1 class="mb-1">Privacy</h1>
<p class="text-muted mb-4">How HomeVault handles your data.</p>

<h4 class="mt-4">What we store</h4>
<p class="small">
    Only what you provide: a username, an email address, a password,
    your name and address, the items you choose to catalog, and any
    photos you upload for those items. Nothing else is collected.
</p>

<h4 class="mt-4">How your email is used</h4>
<p class="small">
    Your email address is used solely to identify your account during
    password recovery — it is never shared, sold, or sent marketing
    material. Password-reset links are valid for 60 minutes and only the
    SHA-256 hash of the token is stored, so a database leak alone cannot
    be used to reset anyone's password.
</p>

<h4 class="mt-4">How passwords are handled</h4>
<p class="small">
    Passwords are hashed with BCrypt before they are written to the
    database. Plaintext passwords are never persisted and cannot be
    recovered — only verified at login time.
</p>

<h4 class="mt-4">Data and image isolation</h4>
<p class="small">
    Every catalog operation is scoped to the CatalogId stored in your
    auth cookie. Database queries filter by that id, and uploaded photos
    are served only by an authorized action that re-applies the same
    filter, so a user cannot see or modify another user's items or
    photos even with a crafted request.
</p>

<h4 class="mt-4">Third parties</h4>
<p class="small">
    HomeVault uses no third-party analytics, advertising, or tracking.
    No data leaves the application's own database.
</p>